[{:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-109500"] :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$\n- $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$\n- $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{2}$ of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ Recall that an *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$ \n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nFirst, we compute $A-2I:$\n\n$$\nA-2I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNext, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A-2I:$\n\n$$\n(A-2I)v=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following map:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{\\,A-2I\\,\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, we see that $A-2I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $1$ step.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ (a.k.a. an eigenvector) corresponding to the eigenvalue  $\\lambda =2$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-109508/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "45c78bd1-f8ca-5d21-