n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nFirst, we compute $A-3I:$\n\n$$\nA-3I=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A-3I:$\n\n$$\n(A-3I)v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A-3I:$\n\n$$\n(A-3I)w=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Again, since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $x$ under the action of $A-3I:$\n\n$$\n(A-3I)x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A-3I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A-3I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}\\overset{A-3I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A-3I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $3$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-109632/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [