-109585/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-109585" :question/id #uuid "7659191f-b5f5-5d8e-9baa-bdc8ca17c847" :question/math-academy-id "q-109585" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ of $A$\n- $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ of $A$\n- $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nFirst, we compute $A+2I:$\n\n$$\nA+2I=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A+2I:$\n\n$$\n(A+2I)v=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A+2I:$\n\n$$\n(A+2I)w=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A+2I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-109585/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "76e60bc4-ae61-5c39-ad80-14e0cdab14da"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-109585"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-9054"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070419/assets/bff904851d01e984d3b1719623d6e4aa4fbde9f070cb4e3fa4121f96a55a34bf.png)\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $\\mathbf v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=4$ of $A$\n\n- $\\mathbf{v}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=4$ of $A$\n\n- $\\mathbf{v}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=4$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* $\\mathbf{v_2}$ of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda$ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_2 \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{v}_1 \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{v}_1 \\neq \\mathbf{0}.$ Recall that an *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$ \n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nFirst, we compute $A-4I{:}$\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070419/assets/c7bc0b1331127f20b8121b32111c152029c75bd0c6b7ebb0abab5425445285f1.png)\n\n- Next, we find the image of $\\mathbf{v}$ under the action of $A-4I{:}$\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070419/assets/a1d998bfdf9ba31174396de548e12e0fae07fdbfa5c2a0e62b0c90ba8071ba64.png)\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $\\mathbf{w}$ under the action of $A-4I{:}$\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070419/assets/bcf76f04db85e4207c2b26e2a7848812c631330f3d65129e93430df1e71592f6.png)\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070419/assets/17eb9f4a4768401d60cdd40aad65bb184799d5c718e1ff97a2204f48a0a8b46b.png)\n\nHere, we see that $A-4I$ maps $\\mathbf{v}$ to $\\mathbf{0}$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\qquad$ *$\\mathbf{v}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=4$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-9056"] :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }0 \\\\ x\\overset{A-5I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A-5I\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the action of the matrices $A+3I$ and $A-5I$ on the nonzero vectors $\\mathbf{u},\\mathbf{v},\\mathbf{w},\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf y$ as depicted above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-3$ of $A$\n\n- $\\mathbf{u}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-3$ of $A$\n\n- $\\mathbf{y}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=5$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* $\\mathbf{v}_n$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda$ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_n \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{v}_{n-1} \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\ldots \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{v}_1 \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{v}_{n-1}, \\mathbf{v}_{n-2},\\ldots, \\mathbf{v}_{1} \\neq \\mathbf{0}.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v} \\xrightarrow{A+3I} \\mathbf{w} \\xrightarrow{A+3I} \\mathbf{0},\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+3I$ maps $\\mathbf{v}$ to $\\mathbf{0}$ in $\\color{red}2$ steps, \nthe vector $\\mathbf{v}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank ${\\color{red}2},$ not $1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} \\xrightarrow{A+{\\color{blue}3}I} \\mathbf{v} \\xrightarrow{A+{\\color{blue}3}I} \\mathbf{w} \\xrightarrow{A+{\\color{blue}3}I} \\mathbf{0},\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+{\\color{blue}3}I$ maps $\\mathbf{u}$ to $\\mathbf{0}$ in $\\color{red}3$ steps, \nthe vector $\\mathbf{u}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $\\color{red}3$   corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda={\\color{blue}-3}$ of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{y} \\xrightarrow{A-{\\color{black}5}I} \\mathbf{0},\n$$\n\ni.e. $A-{\\color{black}5}I$ maps $\\mathbf{y}$ to $\\mathbf{0}$ in $\\color{red}1$ step, \nthe vector $\\mathbf{y}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $\\color{red}1,$ not $2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-9057"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070419/assets/f1a51fd3bae168f0b61e564cc421b2258c70351684c0480538b09ead5763aacd.png)\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $\\mathbf v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=1$ of $A$\n\n- $\\mathbf{v}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=1$ of $A$\n\n- $\\mathbf{v}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=1$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* $\\mathbf{v_n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda$ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_n \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{v}_{n-1} \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\ldots \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{v}_1 \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{v}_{n-1}, \\mathbf{v}_{n-2},\\ldots, \\mathbf{v}_{1} \\neq \\mathbf{0}.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\n- First, we compute $A-I{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070419/assets/d07685a00b1e304d31fdf1b2c4067d8bf521023c448c2ccae8e918d269159ec6.png)\n\n- Next, we find the image of $\\mathbf{v}$ under the action of $A-I{:}$\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070419/assets/ff5818e172da1edc5b12e07f16b232964cc9b051a22498949119f5ae536e3ef8.png)\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector, we now compute the image of $\\mathbf{w}$ under the action of $A-I{:}$\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070419/assets/f7450b8ba8ba29d97b413cb50d429cfb1a9aa534d5908e1917b426068cbb1bdf.png)\n\n- Again, since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $\\mathbf{x}$ under the action of $A-I{:}$\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070419/assets/27c1a886c0b7cb4c4dfa427de1dcb44da5a98d70c24dd80a877d62f7ff9114e5.png)\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070419/assets/1a9cac337deaeaffeafb270281404e3a1917abcf85914fd8b737df8d89a3af01.png)\n\nHere, we see that $A-I$ maps $\\mathbf{v}$ to $\\mathbf{0}$ in $3$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\qquad$ *$\\mathbf{v}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=1$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
